{
  "result": "At dawn, Mira found a key beneath the basil pot, though she had never owned a lock worth opening. The key was small, green with age, and warm from the sun despite having slept in cool soil. She held it against her palm and listened. Somewhere beyond the apartment walls, a bell rang once. Then a second bell answered, farther away. Mira laughed quietly, because the city rarely answered anyone. She tucked the key into her pocket, watered the basil, and decided not to go to work. Instead, she followed the bells downhill toward the river, carrying no map whatsoever.\n\nThe morning traffic moved around her like a school of silver fish, impatient and shining. At each crossing, Mira waited for the green man to appear, then crossed slowly enough to annoy everyone behind her. She wanted the day to notice her absence. Near the bakery, the bell sounded again. A pigeon lifted from a gutter, carrying a red thread around one foot. Mira followed it through an alley smelling of oranges and rain. There, painted on a blue door, she saw a question: \u201cWho keeps the lost hours?\u201d Mira touched the door. It opened inward without complaint or hesitation.\n\nInside was a room full of clocks, none of them agreeing. Some ticked backward, some whispered, some held their hands over their faces like embarrassed children. An old woman sat beside a kettle, knitting a scarf from strands of blue light. \u201cYou\u2019re late,\u201d she said. Mira checked her pocket, but the key had become a compass, its needle turning toward the woman. \u201cLate for what?\u201d Mira asked. The woman smiled, poured tea into cups, and pointed to a clock with no numbers. \u201cFor choosing,\u201d she said. \u201cEveryone arrives eventually. Few admit they have been invited.\u201d Mira lifted the cup. Tea tasted like thunder.\n\nFor a while neither woman spoke. Rain tapped the high window in careful syllables. Mira watched the numberless clock. Its single hand moved in circles, then stopped whenever she looked directly at it. \u201cWhat does it choose?\u201d she asked at last. The old woman laid down her needles. \u201cIt chooses nothing,\u201d she said. \u201cIt shows what you refuse to choose.\u201d Mira remembered the email waiting unanswered, the brother she had not called, the paint brushes drying in a jar. She remembered wanting to leave her safe little life, and wanting someone else to give permission. The clock began to chime softly.\n\nEach chime opened a picture in the air. Mira saw herself at seven, burying a marble beneath her grandmother\u2019s porch. She saw herself at nineteen, boarding a train alone, then stepping off before departure. She saw an unopened letter, a nearly spoken apology, a yellow dress unworn in summer. The scenes did not accuse her. They simply waited, bright and patient as windows across a dark street. \u201cMust I repair everything?\u201d Mira asked. \u201cNo,\u201d said the woman. \u201cBut you must decide which unfinished thing deserves your next honest hour.\u201d Outside, the bells fell silent. Mira held the compass until its needle settled.\n\nIt pointed not toward a door but toward the ceiling. Above the clocks, a narrow staircase unfolded from shadow, each step made of a different wood. Mira climbed. The woman did not follow. \u201cWill I see you again?\u201d Mira called. \u201cOnly if you stop looking for me,\u201d came the answer. At the top, Mira found a rooftop garden suspended above the city. Tomatoes glowed beside moonlit lavender. A boy in a yellow coat sat on the ledge, dangling his boots over empty air. \u201cAre you the keeper of lost hours?\u201d Mira asked. He shook his head. \u201cI am the finder of spare minutes.\u201d\n\nHe offered her a paper cup filled with seeds. \u201cPeople drop them everywhere,\u201d he explained. \u201cWhile waiting for elevators, pretending to listen, staring at screens. Spare minutes are small, but they grow strange things.\u201d Mira looked into the cup. The seeds resembled flecks of gold, ash, and pepper. \u201cWhat should I plant?\u201d she asked. The boy shrugged. \u201cSomething you will return to.\u201d Mira thought of the blank canvas in her closet. She thought of her brother\u2019s number, memorized yet unused. Then she chose three seeds and pressed them into a pot of black soil. Immediately, a green shoot curled upward, reaching.\n\nIts leaves opened into tiny mirrors. In one, Mira saw her brother in his kitchen, attempting pancakes with their father\u2019s old pan. In another, she saw her canvas waiting behind coats and winter boots. A third mirror showed nothing except Mira\u2019s face, older perhaps, smiling without asking permission. Wind moved through the garden, carrying the scent of bread from streets below. \u201cDoes the plant tell me what happens?\u201d she asked. \u201cNo,\u201d said the boy. \u201cIt tells you what remains possible.\u201d Mira reached toward a mirror. Her finger passed through its surface like water. Away, a telephone began to ring insistently.\n\nMira woke beside her basil pot with afternoon sunlight across the floor. For several seconds, she thought the whole journey had been a dream, the kind that dissolves under coffee. Then she felt soil beneath her nails and found three gold flecks in her pocket. Her phone was ringing on the counter. The screen showed her brother\u2019s name. Mira stood very still, listening to the familiar sound. On the fourth ring, she answered. \u201cHello?\u201d she said. Her brother exhaled, then laughed nervously. \u201cI was making pancakes,\u201d he said. Mira looked at the blue door across the alley. \u201cSave me one.\u201d\n\nAfterward, she went to work and apologized for nothing more dramatic than being absent. Her manager blinked, then handed her a stack of forms. Mira completed them carefully, leaving one small corner blank. At lunch, she bought a cheap canvas, two brushes, and paint colored like storm clouds. She carried them home beneath an ordinary sky. On her windowsill, the basil had grown one unfamiliar flower, blue as a door, with a brass key curled at its center. Mira did not pick it. She opened a notebook instead and wrote: Tomorrow, call again. Tomorrow, paint badly. Tomorrow, leave room for bells.",
